The bug is that both `positive` and `negative` are initialized to `nums[:0]`, which means they share the same underlying array as `nums`. When you `append` to `positive`, it writes into the shared backing array, corrupting the data that `negative` (and the original `nums`) still references. Since both slices point to the same memory, appending to one overwrites elements that the other slice is still reading from or will read from.

The fix is to allocate separate slices for `positive` and `negative` so they don't share the same underlying array.

